        "CopyrightInformation": "This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "In this issue of Acta Physiologica, Lilja et al. (1) provide novel insights into the effects of consuming high-doses of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) on exercise-induced muscular adaptations. NSAIDs are routinely administered to relieve symptoms associated with delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) and restore normal physical function following intense physical activity. Millions worldwide report the daily intake of NSAIDS, and their usage is especially common in athletes and those who partake in rigorous exercise (2) . This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."
